"A contour line joins points that are the same height above sea level. Imagine an island in the middle of a calm sea. The edge of the water traces a line all the way around the island, and every point on that line is at a height of zero. That line is the coastline, and it is also the first contour line.": "7f80b19c4228",
"A map is flat, but the land is not. Some places are high and some are low, and a hiker, a farmer, or a person choosing where to build a house needs to know which is which. How can a flat sheet of paper show a mountain? One clever answer is the contour line.": "d7febb885e36",
"A map is flat, but the land is not. Some places are high and some are low, and a hiker, a farmer, or a person choosing where to build a house needs to know which is which. How can a flat sheet of paper show a mountain? One clever answer is the contour line. A contour line joins points that are the same height above sea level. Imagine an island in the middle of a calm sea. The edge of the water traces a line all the way around the island, and every point on that line is at a height of zero. That line is the coastline, and it is also the first contour line. Now imagine that the sea rises by 100 meters. The water's edge moves up the mountain and traces a new, shorter loop. Every point on it is 100 meters high. Let the water rise another 100 meters, and another, and each time draw the new edge. When you look down from above, you see rings inside rings. The difference in height between one line and the next is called the contour interval. The spacing of the lines tells you the shape of the land. Where the lines crowd together, the ground rises quickly over a short distance, so the slope is steep. Where the lines are far apart, the ground rises slowly, and the slope is gentle. A small closed ring in the middle marks a summit. Two summits with a low dip between them make a saddle, and lines that bend into a V show a valley. Mapmakers make contour maps easier to read in a few ways. Every fourth or fifth line is usually drawn thicker and labeled with its height, so you do not have to count all the lines. Many maps also color the land by height. The height of a mountain is measured from sea level, so countries choose an official starting point. Taiwan's heights are measured from the average sea level at the tide station in Keelung. With practice, you can read a contour map like a picture. You can tell which path to the top is shorter but steeper, where a river will flow, and what you would see if you cut the mountain in half and looked at it from the side. That side view is called a profile. Contour lines turn the ups and downs of the land into lines that a flat map can hold.": "85f815befa7f",
